<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The main Plainsong.app bundle's policy.

  `com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation` was here and is gone.
  Library validation is only disabled to load code signed by someone else, and
  the Electron main process loads none: no package in the production dependency
  tree ships a .node binary, nothing under electron/ requires one, and the Rust
  sidecar, the Speech helper and the shortcut helper are separate processes with
  their own signatures and their own entitlement files. Everything this process
  does load — the Electron Framework, Squirrel, Mantle, ReactiveObjC — is
  re-signed with the same Team ID, which library validation accepts.

  It mattered because of what this particular bundle holds. The app carries the
  microphone, Apple Events and (at runtime) the Accessibility grant that lets it
  inject keystrokes into every application. Disabling library validation on THAT
  signature turns a notarized Plainsong into a loader for someone else's dylib
  under all of it — and notarization cannot be retracted by shipping a later
  version. This is the same reasoning the electronFuses block in
  electron-builder.yml is written from.

  If a packaged smoke test ever fails to launch because of this, the fix is to
  find the unsigned library and sign it, not to restore the entitlement.

  `com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars` is here because TCC
  attributes the calendar helper's request to the RESPONSIBLE process, which is
  this app, and under the hardened runtime it refuses to prompt for a process
  whose signature lacks the matching entitlement. 0.9.0-beta.6 shipped with the
  entitlement on the helper only: EventKit answered "no" about ten
  milliseconds after Connect, macOS stored no decision and showed no prompt,
  and the button appeared to do nothing. The grant itself has always been keyed
  to this app's identity (it is what System Settings lists), so carrying the
  entitlement here does not widen what any grant covers; it only lets macOS
  ask. Calendar READING still happens only in the helper, which keeps its own
  copy of the entitlement as the accessing process.

  `com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook` is here for the same
  reason: the same helper reads contact names when the user turns on Contacts
  names for dictation, and macOS asks on this app's behalf.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
  <dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.device.microphone</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.addressbook</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.temporary-exception.apple-events</key>
    <array>
      <string>com.apple.systempreferences</string>
      <string>com.apple.finder</string>
    </array>
  </dict>
</plist>
